RSX draw engine, cellGcm and NV3089: what Soulcalibur II HD's frame needs, and the speed to run it at 60 fps.

- Display lists are copied when the guest stores `put` (a hook in vm_write32), up to their RET: the game
  double-buffers them assuming the RSX is at most a frame behind and queues a dozen frames while the walker starts
  or is stuck in present().
- The supersampled scene is resolved by the NV3089 2:1 tile blits into the texture the final pass reads. The
  resolve is a GPU copy (optional backend call color_target_copy) into a colour target the engine keeps for that
  guest image; guest memory gets the pixels only when anything else touches those bytes (a texture of another
  shape, a 2D transfer, a surface seed or a depth store). RSX_ENGINE_GPU_RESOLVE=0 stores every resolve as before.
- RSX_ENGINE_SNAP_DIR=<dir> (+ RSX_ENGINE_SNAP_EVERY_S) writes the presented frame to PPM.
- The whole surface is read back once per picture and the resolve tiles and front-buffer bands are served from that
  copy until anything is drawn, cleared or written. (Releasing a stale depth snapshot is the toolkit's, #502.)
- NV3089: an unscaled 32-bit blit is copied row by row (vm_memcpy_to) instead of two accessor calls per pixel.
- The vertex program's length and input mask are kept until its start changes or a SET_TRANSFORM_PROGRAM word
  arrives (its hash is memoized by the toolkit, #459); surface pixels are stored into guest memory a 32-bit word at a
  time.

